Global exception middleware operates at the HTTP level and has no visibility
into action execution details.

If action-level context is required, ExceptionFilters are the correct boundary.

In the absence of MediatR, ExceptionFilters should translate downstream
exceptions into Result.Failure objects enriched with action context,
and then delegate serialization to global middleware.

With MediatR, exception behaviors normalize all downstream failures
into Result.Failure at the application boundary,
ensuring controllers handle only deterministic results and no exceptions.




1. If a request flows from the UI through MediatR into the Application layer,
   all downstream exceptions are observable by MediatR behaviors,
   provided those behaviors are registered.

2. If services are injected directly into the UI layer without MediatR,
   downstream exceptions are only observable by MVC actions,
   middleware, or exception filters.

3. In background services or message-queue processing,
   exceptions are not observable by UI-level mechanisms.
   A dedicated execution boundary must exist at the worker/consumer level
   to handle failures consistently (retry, DLQ, compensation, alerting).

Architectural invariant:
Every execution model must define exactly one global failure boundary.
